AL5 prerelease

The plugins framework is disabled by default in the AL5 prerelease. It's an experimental feature which can be tested by users who are interested.

To enable the plugins framework, set the global environment variable 'IMAS_AL_ENABLE_PLUGINS':

export IMAS_AL_ENABLE_PLUGINS=TRUE

We can distinguish 2 categories of plugins, the Access Layer core plugins and the user plugins.

Core plugins are plugins used by the Access Layer Low Level for required core functionalities. In this prerelease, there is no core plugin (core plugins are currently developed). Core plugins will be active in the first AL5 release.

User plugins are (optional) plugins which can be bound manually to the Access Layer library to extend its features. In this prerelease, some user plugins are ready for testing. The procedure to execute these plugins is documented below. Users can develop their own plugins and test them using a similar procedure as described below.     


A. GENERAL REMARKS

In order to compile and try these plugins examples, you need to set the global variables AL_BUILD and IMAS_AL_PLUGINS.

1. Compiling plugins, creating plugins shared libraries

As usual, AL_BUILD is the location of the Access Layer main directory which contains the 'ccpinterface' directory. 

To compile all the examples:

make -f Makefile_examples

You can also compile only one example, for instance to compile the 'creation_date_plugin' example :

make -f Makefile_examples test_creation_date_plugin


2. Creating the directory containing the plugins shared libraries built in step 1

Create a directory which will contain the plugins shared libraries, for example:

mkdir $HOME/access_layer_plugins

Move all the shared library .so files built in step 1 in this directory.


3. In order to use the plugins shared libraries (.so files), you need to set the IMAS_AL_PLUGINS global variable. This variable is used by the Access Layer library to find the available plugins shared libraries.

IMAS_AL_PLUGINS is the directory created in step 2 and containing the plugins .so shared libraries built in step 1. For example:

export IMAS_AL_PLUGINS=$HOME/access_layer_plugins


4. Testing plugins using Fortran programs:

The command:
make test

will compile all fortran test programs which use the plugins compiled in step 1.


5. Plugins examples are described in the PDF file 'plugins_examples.pdf'


B. TESTING THE PLUGINS EXAMPLES

First, compiling all plugins and Fortran/C++ clients:

make -f Makefile_examples
make test


1. TESTING THE DEBUG PLUGIN

This plugin is described in the PDF file. It can be executed on an existing pulse file.

Using C++, the following command:

./test_debug_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0/

will execute the 'debug_plugin' for pulse 54914 whose the 'master.h5' file is located in directory $WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0.

This will call the 'debug' plugin (using the get() operation)  for pulse 54914, run 0, at the specified path.

All examples below use HDF5, so the specified path should be a directory containing the 'master.h5' file. 

Or using Fortran:

./test_fortran_debug_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0/

Or using Python:

python test_debug_plugin.py imas:hdf5?path=$WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0/

2. TESTING THE PATCH_READER PLUGIN

This plugin is described in the PDF file. It will create a new pulse file.

Using C++, the following command:

./test_patch_reader_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0

will execute the 'test_patch_reader_plugin' for pulse 55000 whose the 'master.h5' file is located in directory $MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0.

This will call the 'patch_reader' plugin, creating the pulse file for pulse 55000 at the specified path.

Or using Fortran:

./test_fortran_patch_reader_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0

Or using Python:

python test_patch_reader_plugin.py imas:hdf5?path=$MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0


3. TESTING THE PARTIAL_GET PLUGIN

This plugin is described in the PDF file. It can be executed on an existing pulse file.

Using C++, the following command:

./test_partial_get_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0/

will execute the 'test_partial_get_plugin' for pulse 55000 whose the 'master.h5' file is located in directory $WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0.

This will call the 'partial_get' plugin, reading the pulse file for pulse 54914 at the specified path (folder containing a 'master.h5' file).

Or using Fortran:

./test_fortran_partial_get_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0/

Or using Python:

python test_partial_get_plugin.py imas:hdf5?path=$WEST_PULSE_FILES/54914/0/

 
4. TESTING THE CREATION_DATE PLUGIN

This plugin is described in the PDF file. It will create a new pulse file.

Using C++, execute the following command:

./test_creation_date_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0

This will call the 'creation_date' plugin, writing the pulse file for pulse 55000 at the specified path.
The field 'ids_properties/creation_date' will be patched for the magnetics IDS, occurrence 0. 

Or using Fortran:

./test_fortran_creation_date_plugin imas:hdf5?path=$MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0

Or using Python:

python test_creation_date_plugin.py imas:hdf5?path=$MY_PULSE_FILES/55000/0





